2026-08-29T10:44Z claude note fts5vocab probe of the live fulltext.sqlite, read-only with Zotero running, 2026-08-29. What the 268 MiB holds: 257 463 211 of the 281 280 512 bytes are fulltextContent_data, the packed segment b-trees; 164 916 bytes of _idx, 28 318 of _docsize (which is what makes bm25() work), one _config row at version 4. Unpacked that is 670 680 distinct terms, 19 139 711 (term, document) pairs and 135 973 731 term occurrences WITH POSITIONS. The consequence bears on this ticket. fts5vocab(main, fulltextContent, instance) exposes (term, doc, col, offset) off a contentless table: constrained by TERM it is indexed and instant (all 492 occurrences of permafrost across the library in under a millisecond, returned as itemID plus token offset); constrained by DOC it is a full scan of the 136 M instances, 7,0 s, and identically 7,0 s for a low and a high itemID, so there is no seek. Sorting one document by offset does return readable prose (doc 31 opens "bp statistical review of world energy june 2016 bp com statisticalreview bpstats introduction 1 group chief executive s introduction"), lowercased and stripped of punctuation, but at 7 s per document that is about 25 h for the library: a curiosity, not a route. So the body above says "can serve which items, never which passage", and that is too strong as written — for a QUERY TERM the index yields the item and the token offset for free. Verified rather than asserted, and it half-held: occurrence counts matched fts5 exactly on all three documents checked against their .zotero-ft-cache, so the postings are right, but my token indices drifted +13, +2 and 0 against fts5 offsets. My tokenizer over-splits relative to Zotero(s); cause NOT isolated. The one experiment that settles it: port normalizeForSearch plus unicode61 faithfully and re-check that the drift goes to zero. Until then the offset is real and monotone but not mapped to a character position. Two further facts. The extracted text is on disk regardless: storage/<KEY>/.zotero-ft-cache, 13 631 files, 819,4 MiB of plaintext, with .zotero-ft-info sidecars carrying pdfinfo output — so contentlessness was never the binding constraint on obtaining text from the platform. And action 3 got a free half-confirmation: fulltext.sqlite answered every query with Zotero up, while zotero.sqlite refused the same session with "database is locked". None of this reopens the entry as unit of answer: a token offset is a point, an entry is a span, so the segmenter still runs over the cache text, and reading both index and cache couples us to two undocumented internals instead of one. What it changes is the reasoning the gate rests on — decide it on the coupling and the measured saving (action 1), not on "the text does not come back". CONSTRAINTS.md C2 owns that sentence and is left untouched here; its wording is affected and the edit is the author(s) call.
2026-08-29T10:47Z claude note the .zotero-ft-cache side, surveyed 2026-08-29 on the same library, because the note above makes the cache the text source and its shape was unexamined. 13 631 cache files over 15 908 storage directories, 819,4 MiB, against 13 090 documents in fulltextContent and 14 112 rows in fulltextIndexState — the three counts are not the same population. By attachment content type: 8 590 application/pdf, 4 758 text/html, 10 application/epub+zip, 3 text/plain, 2 xml, and 268 whose key is not in the attachment table at all (orphans). NOT MARKED UP: the caches are plain UTF-8 text. 24 of 13 631 contain tag-like strings and every one is content rather than markup — the largest is an EPUB programming book quoting HTML templates. THE FORMAT HAS EVOLVED, and the split is visible on disk. Restricted to PDFs, 4 708 caches carry U+000C form feeds and 3 882 do not, and the divide is generational by mtime: the form-feed group is 4 695 of 4 708 from 2024 to 2026, the plain group 3 825 of 3 882 from 2019 to 2024. The form feed is a page separator, not decoration: its count equals fulltextItems.indexedPages (n or n+1) for 4 471 of the 4 708, with 237 disagreeing, several of those being the 100-page extraction cap. Mechanism, read in the shipped fulltext.js of build 20260817151751 rather than inferred: indexPDF now calls Zotero.PDFWorker.getFullText and writes the returned text straight through with putContentsAsync, while the poppler pdftotext/pdfinfo binaries are still wired up at lines 280-281 for other callers. Consistent with that, the string zotero-ft-info appears NOWHERE in the 511 shipped files under chrome/content/zotero (positive control: the same grep finds zotero-ft-cache and zotero-ft-unprocessed in fulltext.js), so the 2 788 .zotero-ft-info sidecars on disk are residue of an older pdfinfo-based path, not a current output — 2 526 of them sit with the old no-form-feed PDF group. WHY THIS MATTERS TO THIS TICKET: a segmenter over the cache cannot assume page boundaries exist, because 45% of PDF caches predate the extractor that emits them, and Zotero does not re-extract on upgrade — those caches change only when the attachment is reindexed. Whatever we build on the cache is therefore built on a mixed corpus of two extractor generations, which is a second undocumented coupling on top of the index one, and it belongs in action 5 pricing rather than being discovered later.
2026-08-29T11:11Z claude note the SDT pack, read and parsed 2026-08-29, because C2 names it as the thing to adapt to if the local API ever serves structured extraction and nobody here had opened one. MECHANISM, not heuristics: resource/document-worker ships an ONNX block-segmentation pipeline — clusterer model.onnx 4 362 638 B, repair.onnx 358 150 B, classifier model.onnx 4 367 485 B, on ONNX Runtime WASM 13 479 978 B behind a 5 248 746 B worker.js. Versioned per source type, SDT_PROCESSOR_VERSIONS pdf 3, epub 1, snapshot 1, schema 1.1.0, pack format 1. CONTAINER: magic 0x89 SDT CR LF 1A 0A, then index, metadata, a page catalog, then deflate-compressed chunks of per-block JSON behind an offset table, so a consumer seeks to block n without inflating the file. A 60-line Python reader parses it; the format is ours to read if we want it. THE FINDING THAT MATTERS HERE: every block carries anchor.pageRects [page, x0, y0, x1, y1] and every text run a textMap of per-glyph advances, which is character-level location back onto the page. That is exactly the entry-level location the note above concluded the FTS index cannot serve, so the pack, not the index, is the substrate that could serve the ratified unit of answer. It also carries source.hash (MD5) and a processor version, so staleness is detectable — which the .zotero-ft-cache generations are NOT. QUALITY, and read this as a probe rather than a benchmark: n=2, both PDFs, both mathematical economics. McKenzie Turnpike Theory (JSTOR scan, 26 pages, 343 blocks: 271 paragraph, 47 math, 12 heading, 6 list, 4 image, 3 caption) got all seven numbered sections right plus REFERENCES, and paired three figures with their captions on a 1976 scan; its false positives are an OCR artifact TTT promoted to a heading, the JSTOR cover sheet REFERENCES, and the byline. Concave Generalized Flows (24 pages, 430 blocks) found only FOUR headings — title, 5.5. Analysis., 6.5. Analysis., References — for a paper that plainly has sections 1 to 6, so heading recall there is poor, and two list blocks hold only whitespace. Its math extracts as mojibake, but that is a custom font encoding in the source PDF, upstream of the segmenter and not a classification error; keep the two separate when judging. So: strong on math isolation and figure/caption pairing, uneven on headings, and BETTER ON THE OLD SCAN THAN THE MODERN TWO-COLUMN PAPER, which is the opposite of the intuition and the reason n=2 must not be generalized. TAXONOMY, suggestive only because it comes from string-grepping a minified bundle rather than reading an enum: title, table, paragraph, image, caption, math, header, figure, heading, footnote, footer, list. header and footer as first-class types would mean running heads get labelled instead of dumped into the flow, which is the noise that pollutes flat pdftotext output. THE BOUND IS AVAILABILITY, not quality: 2 packs against 13 631 ft-caches, because the sole caller of Zotero.SDT.getPack in the shipped app is reader.js:1176 — a pack exists if and only if that PDF has been opened in Zotero reader. sdt.js carries an ensure/warm entry point meant for import time and nothing in this build calls it. The better substrate exists for 0,015% of the library. Both packs are dated 2026-08-22 and are the two the recorded zero-pack episode produced, so a future probe that counts packs without first opening a PDF will read zero and mean nothing.
2026-08-29T11:21Z claude note body corrected on the author instruction, not silently: the "text does not come back / which items, never which passage" sub-point is replaced by what the measurement supports, action 6 now carries the SDT branch as the other platform substrate, action 7 is the recommendation with four options rather than three, and an exit criterion requires the SDT branch to be answered rather than skipped. The pack reader is committed as bench/sdt_read.py with tests/test_sdt_read.py, so the pack measurements in the notes above are reproducible instead of asserted; it reproduces both packs exactly (343 blocks / 26 pages / 12 headings, and 430 blocks / 24 pages / 4 headings). Its fixture is synthetic by choice — the real packs are not in the repo and cannot be, so the tests build packs byte by byte, which is also the only way to construct the malformed cases. The load-bearing test is the one refusing an unknown pack version: the format is undocumented and has moved once, and the failure that would cost us is not a crash on a new layout but a silent misparse yielding plausible blocks from bytes that mean something else.
2026-08-29T11:42Z claude note /gaze returned REROLL on PR 33 with three blockers, all real, all closed in one commit; the reader moved to verification/probes/sdt_read.py when the merge with origin/main brought in the verification/ convention (CLAUDE.md, commit 214d4f2), which is where a platform-probe script belongs — the log entry above naming bench/sdt_read.py predates the move and is left standing because the log is append-only. The blocker that mattered: block_starts was unvalidated and failed SILENTLY — flattening the series made every chunk hold zero blocks, and the pack parsed clean, raised nothing, returned an empty document. That is precisely the failure this reader exists to prevent and the one this project already hit on these packs. _validate_index now mirrors validateIndexShape from the shipped module rather than inventing a contract. Second: truncation inside the index region escaped the error contract because n derived from len(index) after a slice had silently shortened it, so short files surfaced as struct.error or IndexError instead of SDTError; the length is checked before the slice now and n derives from the declared index_length. Third: STATE.md asserted that SDT sections are not raw deflate and that reading one means vendoring Zotero decoder, neither costed — this branch disproves all of it and the sentence is corrected. All seven new tests were run against the PRE-FIX reader as a positive control and all seven fail there with exactly the reported symptoms (struct.error at cuts 16 and 20, IndexError at 24 and 28, DID NOT RAISE for the zero-blocks case); a guard whose test passes against unguarded code proves nothing. Also taken from the panel dissent preserved in the verdict: C2 "Zotero does not re-extract on upgrade" was an inference presented as fact, and now states the observation and the inference separately.
2026-08-29T11:51Z claude note /gaze round 2 returned ESCALATE, not on quality — it confirmed all three blockers closed, reproduced my positive control exactly (7 failed against the pre-fix reader, 14/14 against the fixed one), re-attacked with vectors my tests do not name (truncation at 17/18/22/30/39, block_starts[0] in {1,2,3,9999,0xFFFFFFFF}, byte_offsets corruption, non-monotonic middle entries on a 3-chunk pack) and found them all refused cleanly, and checked the merge union line by line rather than by grep. The escalation was six open verifiable items, and it was right on all of them including two corrections to my own report. Acted on: the Makefile linted bench/ and tests/ only, so moving the reader to verification/probes/ silently took it OUT of the lint gate — clean today, but the gate had stopped looking, which is the same defect family as this whole PR and is now fixed by adding verification/probes/ to the ruff target; STATE.md still claimed 106 figure pairs and 24 tests where the harness now runs 160 and 38; the SYNC extraction row asserted a measured weakness without carrying the n=2 hedge that every other statement of that finding carries, and now says two packs, a probe not a benchmark, with the disagreement named. Two claims of mine were simply wrong and are corrected rather than defended: the PR body carried one Ticket-ref and not two (0200 was announced in a comment, which is not the body), and the branch was 5 commits behind origin/main where I reported 0 — it was 0 when measured and parallel sessions kept landing. DISPOSITION of the 511-versus-7 492 control discrepancy, which is not an error: the ft-info claim in the note above was scoped to the 511 files under chrome/content/zotero and says so, while the later SDT claim about reader.js being the sole getPack caller was made over the whole 7 492-file app after extracting all of omni.ja. Two scopes, each stated with its own, and the wider grep later confirmed the narrower one rather than contradicting it.
2026-09-03T07:41Z claude note AUTHOR POSITION on the extract question, recorded 2026-09-03 from the session that re-read this ticket. It changes what action 7 has to choose between; none of it is measured here. (1) MEASUREMENT NEEDS doudou. Actions 1, 3 and 4 all read the live library, and 0503's constraint compounds it: Zotero must stay idle for a build, and driving the local API from a second lane is what produced localApiDegradedAt, so the 0120 measurement slot and a build cannot share a night. Nothing below substitutes for that slot. (2) ZOTERO 6012 HAS MOVED, verified on the PR commit page 2026-09-03 rather than asserted: four commits dated 2026-09-02, cb27b75 'run all sdt extraction before any embedding begins', fbb0495 'approximate chunk token sizes instead of tokenizer', 19e7962 'more polite memory and cpu usage', cb178d9 'minor fixes'. SYNC.md's Zotero core row still carried 'last activity 2026-08-26' and 'has not moved' from the 2026-08-30 at-source checkpoint and is corrected in the same commit as this note; the head SHA and draft state are NOT re-verified at source here. cb27b75 is the one that bears on us: it makes SDT extraction a phase that completes BEFORE any embedding begins, which is the mechanism that would make packs library-wide and is exactly 0572's deferral checkpoint, so that checkpoint is nearer than 0572's text assumes; 19e7962 puts their extraction scheduling under active change, which is the thing our own scheduler is being compared against. (3) THE PLUGIN COMMANDS EXTRACTION BUT NOT THE CAPS. bench/zotero-fulltext-plugin (PR 189) queues per-key re-extraction, which Zotero 10 offers nowhere else, but the caps remain Zotero preferences (fulltext.pdfMaxPages 100, fulltext.textMaxLength 500 000, ticket 0483). X5's uncapped arm got its 2 887 pages by the author lifting the pref by hand: a global, out-of-band change to the user's Zotero configuration, which is not something an unattended tick may do. So truncation is REPORTED first, as 0483 action 3's ledger state fed by the API's own indexedPages/totalPages, and repaired second, never silently. (4) WHAT IS ACTUALLY IRREVERSIBLE is not the extraction but the overwrite: re-indexing through the plugin replaces Zotero's .zotero-ft-cache and bumps the full-text version, so the previous extraction is gone and cannot be compared against, and X6 already observed group-library attachments returning at full-text version 0. Hence the author's rule: do not interfere with Zotero's extraction pipeline as a matter of course. (5) THE AUTHOR'S SHAPE is copy first, then re-extract without caps OVER THE COPY. It keeps the platform artifact intact, puts our write inside our own store, and makes coverage immediate (the copy serves at once) and improving (the uncapped extraction replaces it per attachment). Its cost is a real duplicate in time and space, 819,4 MiB of cache today and more uncapped; the author states the cost is accepted, not that it is small. (6) OUR SCHEDULER IS PROBABLY BETTER THAN THEIRS AT THE MOMENT: an assessment of the author's, not a measurement, and the one claim here a doudou slot could turn into a figure now that 19e7962 moves their side. (7) THE FLAT AND STRUCTURED DUAL CHANNEL STAYS, reaffirmed rather than reopened: pack-first with flat fallback is already ratified as a mechanism under R24 (2026-09-02, ticket 0572). (8) SCOPE RULING added by the author in the same session: zoteus SHOULD help bring the old-generation full texts up to date, but NOT as part of the automatic R1 tick. R1 is about becoming searchable unattended; a stale or capped extraction is a declared quality state, not a coverage hole. So the refresh is an explicitly invoked maintenance verb rather than an unattended duty, and R17 must say how much of the library is served from an old-generation or truncated extraction. NOT SETTLED HERE: nothing above chooses between the index, the pack, both or neither, and no action 1 figure exists. The design change goes to DECISIONS.md first per this ticket's own exit criterion and is filed there awaiting ratification in the same commit, carrying the two questions it cannot answer alone: whether a verb of ours may WRITE into Zotero at all, since the plugin's reindex crosses the read-only posture C2 describes, and whether the uncapped copy is a third channel or a variant of the flat one carrying its own extractor identity.
2026-09-03T07:49Z claude note POINT 8 OF THE NOTE ABOVE IS SUPERSEDED by the author the same day, and the correction is his: a capped extraction is a QUALITY HOLE R1 PROMISES TO FILL, not a declared state a maintenance verb comes to fix. The clause that settles it is R1's second, which the previous note did not weigh: the system MUST NOT need a manual rebuild, whatever state it is in. An invoked refresh verb over 3 882 old-generation PDF caches IS that manual rebuild, so the shape proposed in point 8 fails R1 rather than respecting it. Two things follow that cost nothing to adopt because they already exist. (i) R1's own ladder row already carries 'superseded work draining to the latest chain unattended', so the device is in the requirement: a body extracted under a superseded extraction contract is superseded work and drains newest-first like any other, with no new machinery and no verb. (ii) The bound that keeps this from making R1 unbounded is C1 link 1: the promise is over a DECLARED extraction contract (extractor identity, caps in force), not over 'the best text obtainable', so R1 re-earns a body when the contract changes and not because a better extractor exists somewhere. Without that bound the requirement becomes a treadmill driven by our own extractor version rather than by library change. THE AUTHOR'S SECOND CLAIM, that since we touch neither Zotero's database nor its filesystem the only remedy is to extract ourselves, is VALID BUT ITS WORK IS DONE BY THE RULE, not by the world: the caps are preferences, lifting one writes prefs.js and the re-extraction it enables rewrites .zotero-ft-cache, so every platform-side remedy is a write, and bench/zotero-fulltext-plugin already performs exactly that write today (X5). The inference holds only as long as the no-write rule stands, and it should be recorded as a rule with that consequence rather than as a fact about Zotero. WHAT THE POSITION DOES NOT OPEN, and this is the cheap part: pdf.js is already being vendored for tier 1 (ticket 0560, Apache-2.0, confirmed), the PDF bytes are already reachable without the filesystem through the local API's /file/view/url redirect (tracker 0557), and C1 link 1 already enumerates extractor identities, so ours becomes a third one in a slot that exists. The marginal cost of extracting ourselves is therefore far below what a standalone extract stage would suggest. WHAT IT DOES NOT CLOSE, ranked. (1) THE QUALITY GATE, and it is the blocker: our extractor would be a THIRD generation asserted to beat Zotero's current PDFWorker path, and that is unmeasured; re-extracting 3 882 old caches with something worse is a regression dressed as a fix. X5's eleven attachments are a ready-made control corpus, uncapped and complete on disk with their page counts known, so the arm is cheap once a doudou slot exists. (2) STORAGE STOPS BEING OPTIONAL: C3 streams today and the text is never held, but text we extracted ourselves cannot be asked back from Zotero, so it must be stored, and our store becomes authoritative for text rather than derived-and-discardable. That is a larger architectural move than the phrase 'help bring the old full texts up to date' suggests, and it is where the duplicate cost the author already accepted actually lands. (3) IT IS A PDF ANSWER: 4 758 text/html caches, 10 EPUB, 3 text/plain and 268 orphans stay Zotero-sourced, so mixed provenance is permanent rather than transitional and R17's sentence must carry it. (4) SEQUENCING AGAINST 6012: cb27b75 runs all SDT extraction to completion before embedding, which is what would make packs library-wide, and the pack supersedes a flat re-extraction for exactly the documents we would re-extract, so shipping our own extractor before that lands risks building what upstream obsoletes this month. (5) The granularity widening itself needs a ruling, because it moves what counts as covered and therefore the denominator the convergence harness measures (0026, 0580). The awaiting-ratification entry in DECISIONS.md is amended in the same commit to carry this correction rather than left standing as filed.
2026-09-03T07:55Z claude note MY BOUND IS WITHDRAWN, on the author's push-back 2026-09-03: R1 DOES imply the treadmill, and the entry above was inconsistent in proposing otherwise. The demonstration is C1's own text plus R1's ladder row -- 'superseded work draining to the LATEST chain unattended'. The chain is C1's three links, so when ANY link's identity changes, everything derived under the old one is superseded and drains. That is already true of link 3 and nobody calls it a treadmill: swap the embedder entry and the whole library re-embeds unattended, which is the accepted behaviour the registry work (0488, 0495) exists to gate. Proposing that link 1 alone re-earns only on a contract WE declare gave the extractor an exemption the embedder does not have, and there is no principle that grants it. Withdrawn. WHAT SURVIVES THE WITHDRAWAL is not a bound on what R1 owes but a distinction between two triggers, and it is the whole design object. A bump WE declare -- our extractor version, a chunker geometry, an embedder entry -- is deliberate, dated, one event, and its cost is knowable before it is pressed. A bump that happens TO US is not: SYNC.md's Zotero extraction row already records that a SDT_PROCESSOR_VERSIONS bump silently re-extracts existing packs in the background, so quality moves under us with no signal at all. Under a total supersession rule that unannounced bump drains extraction, chunking and embedding across the whole library on the user's machine, unasked. So the drift watch stops being informational and becomes load-bearing: it is the only thing that turns someone else's unannounced bump into a dated event with a knowable cost. R1 SAYS UNATTENDED, NOT IMMEDIATE, and that is where the treadmill is made survivable rather than exempted: supersession is total, draining is newest-first and paced by the tick and the latency-paced worker (5.2.4), and the index keeps serving the older chain while it drains, which is R4 and D3's serve-stale rather than a new concession. What that forces is a REPORTING obligation, not a scope reduction: R17 must be able to say where the treadmill is -- N items on the latest chain, M draining, which chain is still serving -- or a full drain is indistinguishable from a broken index. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR OUR OWN EXTRACTOR, and it cuts against the cheapness I priced two entries ago: taking ownership of link 1 means every improvement WE make to our extractor re-drains the library through all three links. Zotero's extractor bumps are rare and theirs; ours would be ours, and improving it is exactly what we would want to do. The measured full build is 8 h 14 for 363 613 passages (STATE.md), so an extractor bump is not a cheap event. This does not argue against extracting ourselves; it argues that our extractor needs the discipline the embedder registry already has -- a declared, dated, gated identity whose improvements are batched into rare bumps rather than shipped as they are written -- and it strengthens the quality gate from prudence to cost control, since shipping an unmeasured extractor v1 means shipping v2 soon after, at two full drains.
2026-09-03T07:59Z claude note THE SDT CAP QUESTION IS ANSWERED, on main and not on the installed build: the structured-text path is NOT capped by the full-text preferences -- sdt.js holds no preference or limit at all and getStructuredDocumentText passes no page argument where getFullText passes maxPages. Evidence, scope caveat and the two open controls are at verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md, and the finding is logged in full on 0483, which owns the caps. WHAT IT DOES TO THIS TICKET'S RECOMMENDATION. The fork in the road named two entries ago -- packs uncapped means 6012 delivers the untruncated body library-wide and our own extractor is never needed -- resolves in the direction that FAVOURS WAITING, but only for the truncated half. The reason is the decomposition the same read forced: fulltext.js's preference observers call reindexTruncated, which selects exactly indexedPages < totalPages (and the chars twin) and re-extracts those items unattended, so upstream already owns the truncation ledger AND its drain. An OLD-GENERATION cache is never selected by that query, because a cache fully extracted under the old extractor is not truncated, so the 3 882 pre-form-feed PDF caches remain unreachable by any upstream mechanism. Our own extraction therefore has a NARROWER purpose than the position recorded above assumes: not the capped bodies, which upstream drains on a preference change, but the old extractor generation, which nothing drains. Two datings decide how much of this holds and neither is available from the session container: whether build 20260817151751 carries reindexTruncated at all (grep the extracted omni.ja on doudou), and whether the WASM document-worker imposes a page limit of its own (open 65F79PTJ, 2 913 pages, in the reader and read the pack page catalog). Both are cheap, both need the machine, and action 7 should not be written before them.
2026-09-03T08:22Z claude note THE SESSION'S REASONING, saved at the author's request 2026-09-03 as the discussion this ticket's recommendation will be written from. It is a chain, and two of its links are corrections to positions taken earlier in the same session. (1) THE QUESTION MOVED FROM THE INDEX TO THE TEXT. The ticket asks whether the platform FTS5 index can serve the keyword path; its own earlier notes had already established that the extracted text is on disk regardless, so the binding question is not where the index comes from but WHERE THE TEXT COMES FROM AND IN WHAT STATE. (2) THAT TEXT HAS TWO DEFECTS, and they are not the same defect: capping (pdfMaxPages 100, textMaxLength 500 000) and ageing (3 882 pre-form-feed PDF caches from the extractor generation before 2024). (3) FIRST FRAMING, MINE, WRONG: the refresh as an invoked maintenance verb outside R1. The author opposed R1's second clause -- no manual rebuild, whatever state it is in -- and an operator invoking a refresh over 3 882 caches IS that rebuild. Conceded. (4) SECOND FRAMING, MINE, ALSO WRONG: bound R1 on a declared extraction contract so the widening stays finite. The author's answer is that R1 IMPLIES the treadmill, and he is right for a reason stronger than he needed: the treadmill already runs on C1's link 3, since swapping an embedder entry re-embeds the library unattended and that is the accepted behaviour the registry work gates. Link 1 had no claim to an exemption link 3 never had. Withdrawn. (5) WHAT SURVIVED THE WITHDRAWAL is not a limit on what R1 owes but a distinction between TRIGGERS: a bump we declare is dated and costed before it is pressed, a bump that happens to us (SDT_PROCESSOR_VERSIONS) drains the library with no signal, which is what makes SYNC.md's drift watch load-bearing rather than informational, and what makes R17 owe a sentence saying WHERE the drain is -- otherwise a full drain is indistinguishable from a broken index. (6) THE CONSEQUENCE CUTS AGAINST THE CHEAPNESS I PRICED: owning link 1 means every improvement to our own extractor re-drains all three links at a measured 8 h 14, so our extractor needs the embedder registry's discipline -- declared, dated, gated identity, improvements batched into rare bumps -- and the quality gate moves from prudence to cost control, since an unmeasured v1 means a v2 soon after at two full drains. (7) THE OPERATIONAL OBJECTION, the author's -- this becomes another long-running process to manage -- is answered by the shape rather than by the goal: not a process but a WORK-ORDER KIND in the conductor that already exists (5.2.4's tick dispatches and does not extract, the worker fetches, and the extract shim already carries per-attachment truncation flags), because a separate extraction service would give R22 two pause buttons and is excluded by the requirement rather than by preference. Page-ranged orders, and extraction phase-separated from embedding, which is independently what upstream's cb27b75 just did. (8) THE FORK THAT DECIDED IT: if the SDT packs are capped we need our own extractor regardless; if they are not, 6012 delivers the untruncated body library-wide and the whole process disappears. Read on main 2026-09-03 (verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md, and NOT the installed build): sdt.js holds no preference or limit at all, and getStructuredDocumentText passes no page argument where getFullText passes maxPages. Not capped. (9) THE UNASKED-FOR FINDING THAT REDEFINED THE SCOPE: fulltext.js's preference observers call reindexTruncated, which selects exactly indexedPages < totalPages and re-extracts those items, so upstream already holds the truncation ledger AND its drain, bounded by the ledger rather than by a verb -- the same shape this session reasoned its way to independently. But a cache extracted IN FULL under the old extractor has indexedPages == totalPages, so that query never selects it. (10) WHERE IT LANDS: the truncated half may be upstream's, on a preference change; the structural half is the pack's, when 6012 makes packs library-wide; and the OLD EXTRACTOR GENERATION is ours, because nothing upstream can reach it. Our own extraction is therefore for the generation, not for the caps -- a far narrower purpose than this session started from. TWO GREPS ON doudou GATE THE RECOMMENDATION: whether build 20260817151751 carries reindexTruncated at all, and whether the WASM document-worker imposes a page limit of its own (65F79PTJ, 2 913 pages, opened in the reader, page catalog read with sdt_read.py). Action 1's measurement is still ahead of action 7 and still needs the machine.
2026-09-03T09:31Z claude note Action 2 gets a measured bound, in verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md section 4: for 1 053 attachments the platform FTS5 index holds only the first 100 pages (indexedPages<totalPages, all stopped at the stock pdfMaxPages default), so it cannot serve those bodies at all, whatever its expressive limits. The asymmetry runs the useful way for action 6: exactly where the flat index fails, on long books, the SDT pack is complete, the WASM worker having been shown uncapped over a 2 913-page volume. Caveat bounding every figure: both caps are raised on doudou, so nothing measured here describes a default install.
2026-09-03T09:46Z claude note Route speeds timed on one document in verification/EXTRACTION-ROUTES.md: on the 2 913-page IPCC volume, reading the platform cache over the local API takes 0,8 s, pdftotext 13,3 s, Zotero flat parse 63,7 s, Zotero SDT 554 s. Reading beats parsing by about 70x, which is why the build reads and never parses; the corollary is that the platform's extraction policy, not our throughput, decides coverage. The flat-vs-SDT ratio reproduces on a second document (5,0x on GMAP993G against 8,8x here). Extract and chunk together are 0,21 percent of the 8 h 14 build, so the embedder is the build.
2026-09-03T10:56Z claude note ACTION 1 IS MEASURED, and the figure the ticket has been reasoning around since 2026-08-29 now exists: the whole keyword index over the real library costs wall **305,9 s**, peak RSS **730,6 MiB** and index **709,9 MiB** on disk, over 363 613 passages of 7 541 items. Artifact bench/results/0120-keyword-build/keyword-build-cost.json, note verification/KEYWORD-BUILD-COST-0120.md, assembler bench/summarize_0120.py, three run logs named in the artifact. Same server, same library, same flags as the 2026-09-02 build (sqlite backend, uncapped --max-items, --max-chars 200000); the single difference is ZOTEUS_EMBEDDINGS, and the status object says embedder=none (keyword-only), vectors=0. Set against 29 643,9 s for that embedding build over an identical 363 613 passages, the keyword half is **1,03 %** of that build's wall clock, 30,3 percent of its peak RSS and 43,6 percent of its index. THE PLATFORM SIDE IS A REAL ZERO: no build pass, no extraction, no memory, no bytes of ours, because Zotero maintains fulltext.sqlite whether or not we open it -- a 294,8 MiB file already on this disk. WHAT BOUNDS THE SAVING, and it is the finding rather than the headline: dbstat splits our index into 534,0 MiB of stored passage text and addressing against a keyword index proper of **175,9 MiB**, 24,8 percent. The platform tables are contentless, so they can stand in for the second number and nothing else -- our passage text stays on disk whatever indexes it, since the entry is the ratified unit of answer and fulltext.sqlite cannot print a passage back. So the whole platform path offers at most 175,9 MiB of disk against a larger file we would not control, and a share of a wall clock that is itself one percent of a build. THE ATTRACTION OF THE PLATFORM INDEX WAS NEVER GOING TO BE BUILD COST; if it is worth adopting, the case is what it answers, its staleness, or its coverage, and each of those is another action's question. TWO POINTS RATHER THAN ONE, because a single total cannot be read: 300 and 1 200 items give marginal **0,364 ms** per passage and fixed **133,1 s** per build, the fixed term being the 8 037-attachment walk that every run pays whole. That fit under-predicts the full library by 13,2 percent and the error is reported rather than tuned; the measured 305,9 s is the number to carry. PEAK RSS SITS AT C3's ~750 MB rather than inside it, which is worth flagging even though which C3 row owns a build peak is not this action's to settle. AN INSTRUMENT DEFECT FOUND AND CORRECTED MID-RUN: run_build.py sleeps --poll between status calls and breaks on the first poll that sees done, so elapsed_s is the true time rounded UP to the next poll boundary. At --poll 20 the 300- and 1 200-item runs BOTH returned 140,3 s -- the quantum, not the build -- and a first full-library run at --poll 30 read 332,9 s against the 305,9 s here. Every arm was re-driven at --poll 5 and 332,9 s is superseded. Reported to the goal-1 lane, which had specified in SPEC.md 5.2.9 that timing the convergence harness's existing 1 Hz polls would yield a latency distribution at no additional cost; that is false for the same reason, and the claim is being walked back on worktree-dissolve-c4. INCIDENTAL, FOR ACTION 3 AND NOT A MEASUREMENT: a read-only sqlite3 open of fulltext.sqlite with Zotero up returned 'database is locked' twice about a minute apart. Cause not isolated, no positive control, one unconditioned failure measures nothing -- C2's sentence that live readability is not established still stands as written.
2026-09-03T11:00Z claude note SCHEMA FACTS read off the built index, for actions 2 and 4 rather than for action 1. Our keyword table is EXTERNAL-CONTENT, not contentless: passages_fts is fts5(text, content='passages', content_rowid='pid', tokenize='unicode61 remove_diacritics 2') over a passages table holding pid, id, item_key, title, text, source, vector. So the index is contentless in storage but points back at a store we keep, which is what lets snippet() work and an entry be printed; fulltextContent has nothing behind it in the database, its text living in the cache files. That asymmetry is the mechanism behind the disk bound above and is worth stating in those terms rather than as a size ratio. The store holds 363 613 rows averaging 1 156,4 characters, 401,0 MiB of raw text, against 534,0 MiB for the table plus its three indexes. AND THE TOKENIZERS DIFFER IN A WAY ACTION 4 WILL HAVE TO PRICE: ours folds diacritics at level 2 where the body records fulltextContent as plain unicode61, so the two disagree on which queries match before any offset question arises. WE ARE NOT THE EXTRACTOR, which the action-1 framing can obscure: the build reads Zotero's .zotero-ft-cache text over the local API and never parses, which is why it is cheap and why the platform's extraction policy rather than our throughput decides coverage.
2026-09-03T11:02Z claude note THE AUTHOR'S OBJECTION, 2026-09-03, and it corrects the note rather than qualifying it: the platform's extracted text is truncated in pages and in chars and is never upgraded, so a build that reads it inherits that. He is right, and the first draft of verification/KEYWORD-BUILD-COST-0120.md carried a caveat that read the wrong way -- it said the raised caps on this machine make the body text LONGER than a default install holds, which is true only for what was extracted after they were raised. 0483 measured the rest on 2026-09-03: 1 053 attachments still sit at exactly 100 indexed pages under raised caps, because reindexTruncated fires on a live preference change and nothing has fired it; and 3 882 PDF caches are pre-form-feed old-generation, which that query structurally cannot select since a full old extraction has indexedPages == totalPages. The same volume held in both libraries reads 10 705 102 B in the group copy against 303 749 B in the user copy. SO THE HEADLINE IS A FLOOR, NOT A PRICE: 305,9 s is what it costs to index the library AS ZOTERO HAS EXTRACTED IT, and a build over complete current bodies would carry more passages, more bytes and more seconds. The projection is not attempted, because the missing text's chunk yield is unknown; naming it would be arithmetic dressed as a measurement. WHERE IT LEAVES THE TICKET, and it sharpens rather than softens: the platform FTS5 index is built from the SAME text, so adopting it inherits both defects exactly and with less recourse -- we can point our chunker at a better source, the SDT pack of action 6 or our own extraction, while keeping everything downstream, where a contentless index we do not own leaves us the text quality we are given and no signal when it changes. The build cost was never going to be the argument for the platform index; the text is the argument against it. Note amended with a section stating this before the caveats.
--- body ---
## Context
Zotero 10 ships a finished keyword index that zoteus does not use. The
platform facts are verified and recorded in SPEC.md §4 C2; this ticket
decides what, if anything, we do about them.

What is on disk today, measured on the author's machine 2026-08-29:
`fulltext.sqlite`, four contentless FTS5 tables, 13 090 content documents.
`fulltextContent.rowid` is the local `itemID`, joined 13 090 of 13 090. A
read-only open of the live file answered a count in 7 ms and a `MATCH` in
8 ms with Zotero running.

What zoteus does instead: it reads `/items/<key>/fulltext` and
`/fulltext?since=` over the local API and builds its own keyword index from
the text. That is why the Zotero 10 move did not break it, where the field
review found three projects broken by reading `zotero.sqlite` directly.

So the keyword half of retrieval may already exist, for free, with no
extraction pass, no build time and no memory budget. That is the gate.

## What makes it a real question and not an obvious win
Three things cut against it, and any recommendation has to answer all three.

**The index locates a term, not an entry.** The tables are contentless, so
the stored column and `snippet()` return nothing and a document cannot be
printed back. That much is measured and stands. What does NOT stand is the
bound this ticket first drew from it, that the index can serve "which items,
never which passage": the postings survive, `fts5vocab(…, 'instance')`
constrained by term returns `(itemID, token offset)` in under a millisecond,
and a query term therefore does locate itself inside an item (log,
2026-08-29; SPEC.md §4 C2 corrected to match). The bound that survives is
narrower and still binding. Our unit of answer is the entry (ratified
2026-08-26), an entry is a span where an offset is a point, and turning a
token offset into a character position requires reproducing Zotero's own
tokenization exactly — an approximation drifted +13, +2 and 0 tokens on three
documents. So the index can serve part of the keyword path, not the path.

**It is undocumented and it moves.** No release note names `fulltext.sqlite`,
FTS5, or the split; the 10.0 changelog says only "Much faster full-text
content searches". It already moved once, silently, at userdata step 127.
Depending on an internal file is exactly the exposure C2 exists to describe.

**Concurrency is weaker than ours.** `journal_mode` is `delete`, not WAL, so
a writer takes an exclusive lock. Reads are cheap but not guaranteed
available, and we do not control when Zotero writes.

## Actions
1. Establish what a hybrid would actually buy. Measure our own keyword build
   (wall time, peak RSS, disk) against zero for the platform path, on the
   same library. A saving nobody measured is not a saving.
2. Determine what the platform index cannot answer that R-items require.
   Work from SPEC.md §3, not from intuition: entry-level location (the
   ratified unit), coverage honesty (R4, R17), the filters, accent and CJK
   behaviour. Anything on that list the index cannot serve is a hard bound
   on how much of the keyword path it can take.
3. Test availability under real write load. Run reads while Zotero indexes a
   large attachment and record failures and latencies. A positive control is
   required: force a writer to hold the lock and confirm the probe reports
   the failure rather than silently returning stale or empty.
4. Compare the tokenizers. `fulltextContent` is unicode61 where ours is
   configured for our own folding rules; state where the two disagree, since
   a difference changes which queries match and is not a performance detail.
5. Price the coupling. If we read it, name what breaks when Zotero moves it
   again, and how we would detect that before a user does. SYNC.md's watch
   on `fulltext.js` and `userdata.sql` (ticket 0060) is the detection half.
6. Weigh the SDT pack as the other platform substrate, because it answers
   what the index cannot. `.zotero-sdt-cache` carries `anchor.pageRects` per
   block and a per-glyph `textMap` per run, which is entry-level location on
   the page, and a source hash plus processor version, so staleness is
   detectable where the `.zotero-ft-cache` generations give no signal at all.
   Read one with `verification/probes/sdt_read.py`. Its bound is availability rather than
   quality: a pack exists only for an attachment opened in Zotero's reader,
   2 of 13 631 on the author's machine at filing. Say what would have to
   change for that to stop being decisive, and price the coupling as in 5 —
   the surface is watched in SYNC.md, and a processor bump regenerates packs
   silently.
7. Recommend, with the reasoning: use the index, use the pack, ignore both,
   or use either behind a fallback that rebuilds our own when the file is
   absent, locked, or schema-shifted.

## Test
An experiment, not a unit test. Build the comparison in the bench harness so
the numbers land in `bench/results/` and can be declared in the figure
guard. Every figure quoted in prose from this work gets an anchor.

## Verification
- [x] The saving is measured on the real library, not estimated
      (2026-09-03, `bench/results/0120-keyword-build/keyword-build-cost.json`)
- [ ] Every R-item the platform index cannot serve is named
- [ ] The availability probe has a positive control that fires
- [ ] The recommendation states what breaks on the next platform move

## Invariants
- The entry as unit of answer is ratified and not reopened here. If the
  platform index cannot serve it, that bounds the ticket's scope; it does
  not reopen the ruling.
- Coverage honesty (R4, R17) survives whatever is decided. An index we did
  not build is still an index we must report on truthfully, including its
  staleness, which we do not control.
- C1 holds: anything derived from the platform index is derived data and
  carries a key, the platform's own `localUserKey` included.
- Nothing here is filed upstream. The PR budget is untouched.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The measured comparison exists in bench/results/
- [ ] The bound (what it cannot answer) is stated against SPEC.md §3
- [ ] Availability under write load is measured, with a positive control
- [ ] The SDT branch is answered, not skipped: what the pack can serve that
      the index cannot, and what its availability bound costs
- [ ] A recommendation is recorded, and if it changes the design it goes to
      DECISIONS.md first
